# Trainer Support: audit and minimal extendable contract

Status: **design/audit, not activation**. Audited WebCap's existing training paths against the
Diffusion Pipe foundation (#682) and Fizgig planner (#693), plus Fizgig's actual
CLI/trainer code as of 2026-10-24. No GPU run or pytest execution is asserted.
This file is intentionally named trainer_support.mds for the requested handoff.

## Decision

**The shared WebCap runner stays the owner of all jobs.** Trainers are local
CLI processes. The backend layer is only a translation boundary for:

1. **Plan:** immutable captured settings/media -> native config, working directory,
   environment, ordered shell commands.
2. **Observe:** native logs/metrics -> WebCap progress, loss samples and an
   evidenced output-run identity.
3. **Checkpoint request:** ask the native trainer to save and exit gracefully;
   no shared guess about the trainer's signal.
4. **Inspect:** native output -> saved LoRA candidates, actual resume points
   and proof of a completed checkpoint.

**No new queue, GPU lane, service, database, framework, dynamic registry,
trainer worker, or state machine.** No wholesale Diffusion Pipe refactor.
A backend is removable without affecting WebCap's public job model.
Implement what the second real backend forces us to abstract, and no more.

WebCap retains: captured Sets, captions, Training Review, action identity,
persisted trainerId, FIFO scheduling, GPU coordination, managed process PID,
global Console, history, Test Generations, Manual Screening, Campaigns, and
LoRA Library. Their existing state/routing contracts remain authoritative.

## Verified findings, ordered by impact

### P0 — Current backend boundary is asymmetric, not yet a common contract

- tool/server/training_backend.py: DiffusionPipeBackend has command_plan,
  h3_command_plan, request_checkpoint_save; FizgigBackend exposes separate
  plan_h3, saved_artifacts, request_checkpoint_save. No shared runnable plan,
  progress/inspection contract, or capability behavior exists.
- backend_for_job still rejects fizgig explicitly; _new_job writes
  trainerId=diffusion_pipe (training_runner.py around 2682–2770);
  start_response has no trainer selector (around 2922–3156).
- #693 is an isolated *planner*, not a working integration. Do not enable
  Fizgig by merely accepting its ID or displaying a selector.

**Action:** first move DP's existing script preparation behind one small
plan seam, without changing emitted command text or execution order.
Implement the same plan shape for Fizgig. Leave generic orchestration alone.

### P0 — Existing resume/pause flow assumes DeepSpeed, including terminal truth

- training_runner.py: _validated_resume_checkpoint and
  _rewrite_resume_latest (1690–1719) depend on global_stepN and latest;
  _checkpointed_stop_run_directory, _latest_checkpoint_mtime,
  _request_checkpointed_stop and _checkpointed_stop_error (2028–2148)
  require the Diffusion Pipe run layout.
- _apply_terminal_job_status, _populate_queued_resume_point,
  _queue_paused_job and _refresh_job (2225–2458) all consume this
  DeepSpeed-specific representation. _history_continue_epoch_plan and
  _finish_queued_resume also read latest (2854–2921; 3291–3343).
- training_history.py: _resume_artifacts, discover_runs,
  resolve_managed_resume, validate_resumable_run_for_path and
  resume_point_from_directory (291–325; 347–525) discover ONLY
  latest/global_stepN checkpoints.
- Fizgig's actual trainer saves NAME-NNNNNN-state with a
  training_state.json **written last as a commit marker**; it pauses through
  output_dir/.pause_requested at the next epoch boundary and returns exit 0.
  Sources: Fizgig src/fizgig/families/train.py (_save_state, pause handling).

**Action:** backend inspection must return a verified opaque resume reference.
WebCap still decides whether Pause or Finish means queue/History; the backend
only requests a checkpointed exit and later proves one actually exists.
Never use DP's latest checks for Fizgig or consider a state-directory name
alone proof of a complete save. Existing pause/restart GPU behavior is frozen.

### P0 — Candidate and loss paths are Diffusion Pipe specific

- training_runner.py: candidate_epoch_folder_path/_candidate_safetensors_path
  (937–957), Copy to Test and Save selection (1123–1305) assume epochN/
  with a single LoRA safetensors inside.
- training_candidates.py: artifact_for_epoch and saved_artifacts_for_run
  (174–208) likewise require epochN directories; read_loss_events (60–95)
  requires TensorBoard train/loss and train/epoch_loss.
- Fizgig saves NAME-NNNNNN.safetensors and a separate
  NAME-NNNNNN-state directory. The isolated adapter now validates
  training_state.json plus required state files, without treating the
  folder name as a complete checkpoint; its actual per-step loss source is
  output_dir/.fizgig_loss_NAME.jsonl, records {s: step, e: epoch, l: loss,
  lr?: rate}, buffered 20 records per flush.
  Source: Fizgig src/fizgig/training/step_log.py.
- This is a **normal format difference**, not a reason to duplicate WebCap's
  candidate scoring, smoothing, candidate selection, or Evaluation UI.

**Action:** an adapter supplies normalized candidate paths and genuine
loss samples. Existing shared analysis consumes them. If required inputs
are unavailable, mark analysis unavailable; do not forge TensorBoard events
or silently manufacture an epoch loss. Do not scan an entire run directory
or JSONL stream on each progress poll.

### P0 — Runtime/preflight and launch remain globally Diffusion Pipe wired

- training_runtime.py configured_training_settings() reads
  training.diffusion_pipe_wsl, the DP activation/Conda runtime and DeepSpeed.
- training_preflight.py build_preflight/build_launch_preflight (199–319)
  check the DP working directory, train.py and deepspeed executable.
- training_runner.py _build_runner_script (1720–1837) emits its specific
  single/hi/lo/H3 cache flow, DP activation and launch paths;
  _launch_job (1861–1931) uses _training_settings() and unconditionally
  rewrites DeepSpeed latest before starting.
- WebCap's managed setsid bash, PID, log and result files are already
  a valid common process supervisor and should stay that way.

**Action:** derive working directory, interpreter/activation, native phases
and required executable checks from the selected plan. Keep the existing
wrapper/PID/results/Console and existing GPU scheduling intact.
Do not add another process manager or an extra launch lane.

### P1 — Capture format is DP-specific; Fizgig mapping is currently narrow

- training_bundle.py materialize_training_bundle (442–598) captures
  source media and captions immutably, but writes DP-style per-stage
  config and dataset TOMLs, with DP-specific image adapter, focused blocks,
  initializer and dataset bucket settings.
- Fizgig's dataset input uses [general] plus [[datasets]], independent
  cache directories, per-dataset repeats, and area/aspect bucketing;
  it is not byte- or behavior-equivalent to DP size_buckets.
- tool/server/training_fizgig.py currently handles image-only, exactly one
  image bucket per source directory. It rejects other captured layouts,
  including clip/audio buckets. Fizgig itself supports clips, sound and voice;
  this is a bridge limitation, not a trainer capability limitation.

**Action:** capture once, then render trainer-specific config from the
captured membership/sidecars. Preserve review selection and repeat weights;
make any unavoidable bucket semantic difference visible in the effective
run settings. Support representative existing H3 image workflows first,
then real video/audio captures without flattening or dropping items.

### P1 — A real Fizgig H3 training default is unsafe for our experiments

- #693 originally omitted photo_blocks unless manually supplied, silently
  allowing all-block image training. The safe follow-up explicitly defaults
  image mode to photo_blocks=20-49; all-block training remains opt-in.
- Fizgig src/fizgig/families/train.py _run_train: empty --train_blocks
  leaves blocks unrestricted (all trainable targets). The actual Fizgig H3
  Default preset uses photo_blocks=20-49, clip_blocks=20-49,
  audio_blocks=20-49; blocks 0–5 are intentionally excluded.
  Source: Fizgig src/fizgig/families/minimax.py, H3_LIKENESS_MODE.
- #693 also explicitly chooses AdamW rather than DP's adamw_optimi;
  that is an intentional different recipe, **not** settings parity.
  LR, image_shift, dropout, warmup, optimizer, network targeting and
  other options must be labeled native/effective, not passed as alleged
  equivalents.

**Action:** specify a deliberate, visible Fizgig H3 recipe (or require
explicit block mode) before its first managed run; never let a missing
setting accidentally imply all-block training. Keep exact effective
commands/config and trainer version with the run for repeatability.

### P1 — Current candidate analysis requires real evidence, not invented parity

- Fizgig logs epoch N/M, average loss, step and s/step and writes per-step
  JSONL as described above. The isolated Fizgig adapter now implements a
  native epoch-log reader and an on-demand JSONL reader. Neither is yet
  wired to the shared runner.
- DP's loss curve analysis uses two TensorBoard scalar feeds with a
  wall-time-based epoch assignment. Fizgig JSONL already has epoch per
  step, but not the same TensorBoard event wall-time semantics.
- Decouple normalized loss ingestion from the existing candidate algorithms.
  Loss coverage or pending points must not be interpreted as zero.
- The final Fizgig LoRA NAME.safetensors is also saved as the final
  numbered NAME-NNNNNN.safetensors; avoid double-counting a final candidate.
  Resumable state directories are NEVER candidates.

### P2 — Capabilities belong to the trainer, not global UI guesses

- Supported H3 Fizgig features include still images, clips/voice, adapter
  selection, block selection, EMA, pause/resume and ordinary LoRA output.
- Not all DP settings mean the same thing and some Fizgig H3-only features
  (e.g. training modes, TREAD, audio) are genuine separate settings.
  Fizgig's H3 does not support its own cross-family per-image loss-watch
  or adaptive-LR features. Do not make those look like common functionality.
- A small static capability declaration can control availability and
  labels. Do NOT invent generic sliders, an extensible parameter schema,
  or a driver/plugin registry just to render a hypothetical third backend.
- Never select backend by inspecting config filenames, output folder names,
  or global defaults. Persist trainerId on the immutable job and copy it to
  history and every continuation. Missing legacy trainerId is Diffusion Pipe.

## Minimum practical contract (four operations; not an API framework)

The shapes below are conceptual Python dictionaries, not an instruction
to add dataclasses, DI, Protocol classes or an interface registry.

    backend.plan(job, captured_inputs, runtime_settings, resume_point=None)
        -> {
            cwd: Linux working directory,
            activate: optional existing environment activation,
            phases: [{name: "cache" | "train" | ..., command: shell command}],
            staged_files: trainer-native config text/paths, if needed,
            output: run identity/evidence anchor
        }

    backend.observe(job, log_tail)
        -> {
            phase: optional phase label,
            epoch: optional actual epoch,
            step: optional actual step,
            loss: optional observed loss,
            lr: optional observed LR,
            seconds_per_step: optional observed timing,
            output_run_path: optional positively evidenced path
        }

    backend.request_save(job, run_dir)
        -> None
        # Requests a checkpoint-safe exit (both Pause and Finish).
        # The shared runner decides when the process actually finished.

    backend.inspect(job, run_dir, *, include_loss=False)
        -> {
            resume_points: [{id: opaque native ref, epoch: optional epoch}],
            candidates: [{
                epoch: saved epoch, path: real LoRA path,
                variant: optional source-defined kind
            }],
            loss_points: optional [{step, epoch, loss, lr?}],
            checkpoint_verified: boolean
        }

- A fixed, explicit backend_for_job lookup is enough for two backends.
- Optionally backend.capabilities is a small static dict/tuple attached
  to that implementation; introduce keys ONLY for features WebCap uses.
- Validation and trainer-specific preflight belong in plan (or one
  backend-specific probe called before plan if needed for UI).
  Generic shell/GPU/disk checks remain shared.
- Do not create backend-level queue state, job status transitions,
  thread loops, independent logs, or GPU reservations.
- A plan contains ordered shell commands, not an entire execution engine.
  The WebCap wrapper runs the phases sequentially, emits phase markers,
  preserves managed PID, and aborts visibly on failed phases.
- The common API uses paths and observations; trainer-native config and
  CLI flags stay inside the implementation.
- No guessed equivalences. Unsupported capabilities either remain
  unavailable in that backend or fail explicitly on request.
- Inspect is used for requested history/candidate/terminal operations;
  observe is deliberately light enough for normal polling.
- For Pause/Finish, call request_save, observe managed process exit, then
  inspect a **fresh, valid checkpoint** before transitioning state.
  Support stop-now as the existing generic process action.
- Resume uses a native opaque point returned by inspect, passed to plan;
  WebCap does not manufacture or rewrite another trainer's checkpoint.
- Keep trainer ID, effective recipe, model identity and output provenance
  in existing job/history records. Do not create a second state store.

## Low-risk forward progress after this audit

The isolated follow-up branch adds **only**:
- an explicit H3 image-block default (20–49), preserving opt-in all-block runs;
- ordered latent/text/train command phases, while retaining the current map;
- a Fizgig epoch-log parser and on-demand native JSONL loss parser;
- inspection of real LoRA paths and committed resumable states, rejecting
  incomplete committed states without claiming process-exit freshness;
- unit tests for these behaviors and the unchanged Diffusion Pipe selector.

It does **not** modify the Diffusion Pipe runtime, preflight, Queue/GPU,
process supervisor, resume protocol, candidate evaluation, or UI. The four-
operation shared contract remains a target; new Fizgig helpers are not
proof that the shared runner has been migrated or end-to-end verified.

## What MUST NOT become an abstraction

- The existing GPU FIFO and queue ownership rules in AGENTS.md.
- Set curation, media review, captions, dataset versioning or screening UI.
- Generic user settings into arbitrarily named CLI arguments.
- DeepSpeed-specific implementation internals as common fields
  (global_stepN, latest, save_quit, --deepspeed).
- Fizgig-specific implementation internals as common fields
  (.pause_requested, NAME-NNNNNN-state, --family_option).
- Training algorithms or backend model semantics.
- A new backend-install/auto-download lifecycle.
- Dynamic imports/registries/configurable method dispatch for two known
  local engines. An explicit backend selector function is sufficient.
- Fake parity in learning rate, batch size, epochs, training modes or
  optimizer semantics.
- Independent candidate analysis code for each trainer.

## Safe, measurable extraction order

**Phase A — Establish baseline and the contract.**
Record actual DP submitted command, runner identity, Pause/Finish behavior,
resume/continue, candidate discovery/copy/selection, History, screening
and queue recovery tests. No DP refactor on untested paths.

**Phase B — Make a single shared LaunchPlan seam.**
Use one DP implementation to produce the existing phases, activation/cwd
and settings. Keep byte-equivalent commands and shell wrapper. Move DP-only
preflight probes out of generic preflight code, preserving messages/results.

**Phase C — Extract true observation and artifact seams.**
Redirect DP progress/log parsing, run identity, checkpoint checks,
candidate enumeration and loss reader through its backend. Keep the
public data shape and all Evaluation algorithms unchanged. Verify legacy
runs without trainerId exactly as before.

**Phase D — Complete Fizgig behind the same contract.**
Reuse the captured image Set. Produce valid native TOML, configure a
deliberate H3 block mode, pinned executable, model paths and independent
cache phases. Use the same managed process and queue. Parse real Fizgig
progress JSONL/logs and inspect state markers/numbered checkpoints.
Feature-gate video/audio until conversion is implemented and tested.

**Phase E — Activate only after an actual vertical slice.**
One image Set: capture -> all caches -> train -> progress/loss ->
checkpoint -> Copy to Test -> Pause -> checkpoint verification ->
Resume -> Finish -> History and restart reconciliation. Test both
adapter Off and an Ostris/Circlestone run when supported encoder works.
Confirm ComfyUI loads and samples the generated H3 LoRA. No silent
fallback to Diffusion Pipe on Fizgig errors.

**Phase F — Expand only where justified.**
Video/audio capture, more native settings and optional advanced trainer
capabilities as real workflows require. Do not delay architecture cleanup
waiting for hypothetical parity.

## Regression requirements / failure-isolation proof

1. Diffusion Pipe commands/config and action captures remain equivalent;
   existing runs continue to load without trainerId.
2. Queue/GPU ordering, PID verification, restart reconciliation and
   pause-state persistence are unchanged.
3. DP checkpoint-safe Pause, Finish, queued Resume, manual old-checkpoint
   rewind, Continue and history state continue to work.
4. DP loss curves and Candidate Analysis, saved epoch selection and Copy
   to Test retain their current results/provenance.
5. Fizgig plan produces valid commands and TOML from real reviewed
   captures; missing supported assets and incompatible sets fail visibly.
6. Failed cache phase never starts training; failure reaches global Console.
7. Fizgig pause requires observed clean process termination and a valid
   new training_state.json; incomplete states do not count as successes.
8. Native save and resume checkpoint references are never confused with
   export .safetensors; no collision between final and numbered epochs.
9. H3 emitted LoRA is verified loadable by WebCap/ComfyUI before enabling
   direct Copy to Test. Error messages identify actual trainer + operation.
10. Removing Fizgig backend code leaves all DP workflows working. No
    trainer-specific conditions or dead flags remain in shared services.

## Source map

WebCap:
- AGENTS.md — KISS and managed GPU/process invariants.
- tool/server/training_backend.py — current asymmetric seam.
- tool/server/training_runner.py — wrapper, state, launch, pause,
  resume, candidates, Evaluation and job creation.
- tool/server/training_runtime.py, training_preflight.py — DP runtime.
- tool/server/training_bundle.py — captured DP configs/datasets.
- tool/server/training_progress.py — DP log vocabulary.
- tool/server/training_candidates.py — DP TensorBoard/epoch layout.
- tool/server/training_history.py — DP resume discovery.
- tool/server/training_fizgig.py — proposed planner and current limits.
- docs/trainer_backend_foundation.md, docs/trainer_fizgig_h3.md.
- https://github.com/alienzed/webcap/pull/682
- https://github.com/alienzed/webcap/pull/693

Fizgig upstream (verified source, not just feature descriptions):
- https://github.com/shootthesound/Fizgig/blob/master/src/fizgig/families/train.py
- https://github.com/shootthesound/Fizgig/blob/master/src/fizgig/families/minimax.py
- https://github.com/shootthesound/Fizgig/blob/master/src/fizgig/training/step_log.py
- https://github.com/shootthesound/Fizgig/blob/master/docs/CLI.md

**Design pass condition:** if Fizgig is deleted tomorrow, this work
still leaves one simpler, well-isolated Diffusion Pipe implementation
and a small, genuine adapter seam for the next CLI trainer.
